.TH Y-NOT 1 "2026-10-20" "y-not 1.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
y-not \- explain why a user can or cannot access a file or directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B y-not
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I USER OPERATION PATH
.br
.B y-not
.RB { \-\-help | \-\-version }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B y-not
traces the access path to
.I PATH
and reports whether
.I USER
can perform
.I OPERATION
on it, and if not, which component of the path blocks access and why.

Instead of piecing together information from
.BR ls (1),
.BR namei (1),
.BR id (1),
and
.BR getfacl (1),
.B y-not
evaluates owner/group/other permissions, supplementary groups, POSIX ACLs,
Linux mount restrictions, symlinks and special files, and reports the result in
a single, human-readable explanation. Capability grants from pam_cap's
capability.conf are used as an approximation; this is not a simulation of an
actual target process's effective capabilities.
.PP
When missing group membership explains a denial, y-not names one group whose
mode bits or effective ACL permissions would grant the requested operation.
For ACLs, the effective permissions after the ACL mask are considered. This
identifies a relevant group, not every possible group membership.
.SH OPERATION
.TP
.B read\fR, \fBr
Can the user read the file, or read directory entries to list their names?
.TP
.B write\fR, \fBw
Can the user write to the file, or create an entry in the directory? Creating an entry requires both write and traversal permission. Whether a particular entry can be removed or renamed also depends on that entry and the sticky bit; y-not cannot decide that without an entry name.
.TP
.B execute\fR, \fBx
Can the user execute the file, or traverse into the directory?
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit a JSON document (schema_version 1) instead of text. Must be the first
argument. Always emits a valid document, even for an unknown user or a
path that could not be resolved.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Access is allowed.
.TP
.B 1
Access is denied, or an error occurred (unknown user, invalid path, bad
arguments).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Check whether alice can read a file:
.B y-not alice read /srv/project/report.pdf
.TP
Same, using the short form:
.B y-not alice r /srv/project/report.pdf
.SH LIMITATIONS
.B y-not
models Unix permissions and POSIX ACLs and applies read-only/noexec mount
restrictions. pam_cap grants are only an approximation of process capabilities;
SELinux and AppArmor status are reported for context, but neither changes the
allow/deny verdict: SELinux policy
evaluation and AppArmor's per-program confinement do not fit into a
single user/operation/path query. Read-only and noexec mount options are
included in the verdict; nosuid is informational. Mount options are read from
y-not's own mount namespace, which may differ from the target process's
namespace. Directory write checks model entry creation (write plus traversal),
not removal or rename of a specific entry; those may also depend on the sticky
bit and entry ownership.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR stat (1),
.BR getfacl (1),
.BR namei (1),
.BR capabilities (7),
.BR capability.conf (5)
